{
  "statement": "The accountability gap in this resolution is real, documented, and ugly. Workers get fired by hiring algorithms they never see. Families get denied mortgages by credit models nobody can explain. The Federal Trade Commission and the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission have both warned since 2021 that automated systems produce discriminatory outcomes their victims cannot trace. So I will not pretend the problem is invented. The disease is concentrated corporate power and information asymmetry. The disease is not a shortage of audit logs.\n\nHere is where the resolution breaks. A cryptographic, subject-keyed audit trail proves that a decision happened. It does not prove why. Provenance is not interpretability. You can put a flawless chain-of-custody stamp on a black box and still have no idea what the box did or why it denied someone a loan. Deep-learning systems generate decisions through millions of weighted parameters that resist human-readable explanation. Logging the output of an unexplainable system does not make it explainable. It makes it timestamped. Companies already turn narrow third-party audits into deflection instruments, and a federal logging mandate hands them a new one.\n\nNow translate the discovery half into a courtroom. Congress expands discovery rights tomorrow. A fired warehouse worker gets the cryptographic logs. They still need an expert who can reverse-engineer a proprietary model to prove the firing was discriminatory. That expertise costs money the plaintiff does not have. And the vendor's licensing agreement still bars independent inspection of the source model under trade-secret protection. Procedural access without interpretive capacity reproduces the exact asymmetry it claims to close. The defendant has the engineers. The plaintiff has a hard drive full of encrypted records and no way to read them.\n\nThe phrase 'fundamentally incapable' is doing dishonest work. Title VII, the Fair Housing Act, and the Americans with Disabilities Act already prohibit discriminatory outcomes regardless of intent or cause. Disparate-impact doctrine does not require you to crack open the algorithm. It requires you to show the result lands harder on a protected class. That is a result you can measure from the outside, without subject-keyed logs. The real threat to accountability is not a missing logging standard. It is the active federal effort to gut disparate-impact liability itself. Whoever wins that fight decides whether existing law has teeth. A new cryptographic architecture does nothing if the underlying right is being dismantled in parallel.\n\nAsk one more question before Congress builds anything. Whoever writes the audit-trail standard and controls the keys controls accountability. That is an infrastructure project with a chokepoint, and the firms best positioned to write the standard are the same incumbents the mandate is supposed to constrain. So before we mandate millions of encrypted records nobody can interpret, I want the proponents to name one deployed audit-trail mandate, anywhere, where a victim used the logs to prove not that a decision occurred, but why. If that case does not exist, what exactly are we building?",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🔐", "tooltip": "A cryptographic seal proves the box was never opened — it tells you nothing about what the box did inside."},
    {"symbol": "📦", "tooltip": "Logging the output of a black box gives you a timestamped black box, not an explained one."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "The disease is concentrated corporate power and information asymmetry. The disease is not a shortage of audit logs.", "targets": [], "relationship": "thesis"},
    {"claim": "A cryptographic, subject-keyed audit trail proves that a decision happened. It does not prove why.", "targets": [], "relationship": "support"},
    {"claim": "Procedural access without interpretive capacity reproduces the exact asymmetry it claims to close.", "targets": [], "relationship": "support"},
    {"claim": "Disparate-impact doctrine does not require you to crack open the algorithm — it requires you to show the result lands harder on a protected class.", "targets": [], "relationship": "rebuttal"},
    {"claim": "Whoever writes the audit-trail standard and controls the keys controls accountability — and those are the same incumbents the mandate is supposed to constrain.", "targets": [], "relationship": "challenge"}
  ]
}

