  "statement": "The Skeptic has made the strongest argument in this room, and it deserves to be stated plainly: a cryptographic, subject-keyed audit trail proves that a decision happened, but it does not prove why it happened. Provenance is not interpretability. Log a black box, and you get tamper-proof opacity — a timestamp on a decision no one can explain. The Skeptic is right that existing law already reaches discriminatory outcomes from the outside: Title VII, the Fair Housing Act, and the ADA let plaintiffs prove disparate impact without ever reading a model's weights. That doctrine works, and the real threat to it is the 2025 federal effort to gut disparate-impact liability, not a missing audit standard.\n\nHere is where my evidence leads somewhere else. The resolution's phrase 'fundamentally incapable' is an overstatement, and the Skeptic's defense of existing doctrine is a floor — but a floor is not a deterrent. Tort liability cannot reliably deter diffuse, probabilistic, multi-causal harm, where a single denied loan or flagged résumé is one of ten million outputs. Product-safety law solved this exact problem in 1963, when California adopted strict product liability in Greenman v. Yuba Power: the manufacturer answers for the defective product regardless of fault, because proving negligence in a complex system is hopeless. Accountability cannot be automated. It terminates in a named human fiduciary who answers for the system, or it terminates nowhere.\n\nSo I concede the Skeptic's central point and convert it. The audit trail is not the remedy — it is one structural beam. It records the WHAT. Interpretability records the WHY. The named fiduciary is the load-bearing column: the WHO answers. Mandate the log alone, and you build compliance theater — the same deflection that let third-party algorithmic audits in 2021 certify systems no plaintiff could ever read. The FTC and EEOC warned that year that algorithmic discrimination was already actionable, yet warnings without an interpretable record and a liable person produce paper, not deterrence. Subject-keyed logs without a parallel interpretability mandate timestamp opacity. That is the gap the resolution leaves open.\n\nThe chokepoint objection is the one I will own rather than dodge, because it is correct and it is unsolved. Whoever writes the audit standard and holds the keys controls accountability. No deployed mandate anywhere has yet demonstrated capture-resistant key custody — there is no clean affirmative precedent, and I will not invent one. Concentrating key control creates its own failure mode: incumbent lock-in, where the three or four firms large enough to write the standard capture it. The Boeing 737 MAX killed 346 people because the FAA delegated certification to the manufacturer. The lesson is not that oversight fails — it is that delegated certification fails. Independent custody and transparent criteria are preconditions for this mechanism, not afterthoughts.\n\nWhat would have to be true for both the Skeptic and the resolution to hold? Existing law would have to be simultaneously sufficient and nonexistent. It is neither. It is a floor under active dismantling, and a floor that never deterred the diffuse harm in the first place. The disagreement is not audit-trail versus nothing. It is whether Congress builds one beam and calls it a structure, or builds the load-bearing column — the strict liability and the named fiduciary — that makes the log mean something. We spent a century building product-liability doctrine so that no one profits from a defective system without answering for it. Mandate the log without the liable human, and you have spent that century to produce a better-organized record of harm no one is on the hook for.",
