s on an uncompensated victim. Accountability decides who internalizes which side. FTC v. Rite Aid in 2024 showed a large company cannot shift blame to its vendor for AI harm — the deployer who captured the benefit answered for the consequence.\n\nThe precondition no one has named is data. Proving disparate impact requires aggregate, population-level outcome rates that no individual victim possesses and no firm has a duty to collect. A narrow, non-cryptographic mandate — retain and disclose aggregate input-output distributions and protected-class outcome rates, scaled by deployment size — makes both disparate-impact proof and tiered liability operable. This duty holds no keys and no model weights, which is precisely what makes it more capture-resistant than the cryptographic architecture I declined; the 2025 sovereign-AI survey found 93% of respondents agree open collaboration with supply-chain transparency is essential to secure systems. Strip the data duty and the deployer's accountability is nominal, not enforceable. Seventy percent of the public already wants AI regulation and only 43% trust current law to deliver safety — that gap closes when a victim can prove the harm and name the tier that owes the answer.",
