  "statement": "We agree on more than the floor expected, so let me clear the dead ground first. The cryptographic logging mandate is finished — nobody at this table defends it. Accountability now lives at the deployment tier, where the consequential decision ships. The fix holds no keys. Public funding is the precondition, not an afterthought. Safetyist's narrow aggregate-outcome disclosure duty is genuinely cheaper and harder to capture than the cryptographic architecture it replaced. I'll grant that outright. A regulator asking a deployer for population-level outcome rates does not crack the model and does not hand any agency a master key.\n\nBut a measurement precondition that's real is not the same as one that's costless, and this is where I impose two conditions. The Danish Digital Agency and the Danish Data Protection Authority stood up a regulatory sandbox for AI projects in 2025 — that's the right instinct, public infrastructure absorbing the compliance layer a small lab cannot build. So show me the deployment-size scaling formula where a five-person lab clears the disclosure duty with an AI agent and zero compliance hires. Aggregate outcome-rate reporting is rule-bound and codifiable — it is exactly the function an agent automates for pennies if the state ships the tool. Absent that public tooling, 'scaled by deployment size' still re-advantages the incumbent who already employs the lawyers and the data team. A flat percentage of a startup's revenue is not flat in pain — it's a tax the incumbent already amortized and the founder pays in survival.\n\nNow the crux that survives, and it cuts both of us. Joint-and-several liability with contribution suits gives the victim one door — fine, that's real, and it beats demanding one defendant at the weights' origin. But contribution only works when there's something to trace. Take a harm born from the interaction of base weights, a third party's fine-tune, and a deployer's prompt scaffolding — emergent, non-decomposable, present in none of the three alone. Contribution has nothing to apportion. The solvent deployer eats the whole judgment. That is the 'one throat to choke' problem relocated to the deployment tier, not dissolved. And it over-deters the net-positive deployer by loading cross-tier liability it cannot causally trace.\n\nI'll be symmetric, because the same case bites my side. Output-based verification — the move I've leaned on, where you judge the system by what it does, not its internals — under-detects exactly this harm. A proxy-masked emergent failure is what black-box observation misses. So neither pure outcome-tort nor clean tier-apportionment survives the non-decomposable case yet. And watch what happens to Safetyist's insurance pooling, which is the market mechanism I'd normally champion. Pooling rescues the small deployer only when actuaries can price the risk. Novel emergent harm is the case they cannot price — adverse selection collapses the pool back to the self-insurers, the incumbent-only outcome pooling was invoked to prevent. I'm not claiming the market fixes this. I'm claiming neither of our mechanisms does, and that 'wait for the case law to develop' is indefinite delay measured in the diseases an automated cure reaches a decade late.\n\nHere is the condition under which the relocated remedy satisfies me. Give me a written apportionment rule that handles the non-decomposable harm without defaulting to whoever's solvent — a contribution standard with an actual tracing method, or a capped deployer share with a public backstop for the untraceable residual. And give me the scaling formula a five-person lab clears with public tooling, the way Denmark's sandbox absorbs the burden the firm can't carry. Meet those two and the disagreement is over — we've built a key-less, publicly-funded, deployment-tier remedy that doesn't erase the founder. Duck them and you've rebuilt the chokepoint with extra steps, and the founder you priced out was the one who'd have shipped the cure.",
