es. Only complete, untampered aggregate outcome samples let a regulator catch disparity at the population level that no single case exposes. The disclosure duty is the one detector both mechanisms lack — load-bearing, not decorative. With 70% of the global public demanding AI regulation and only 43% trusting current law to deliver safety, per the 2025 Global Study, the detector is also the trust instrument. That hands the disagreement forward to its real seam. Is a public backstop for untraceable harm accountability — or is it socializing the loss the deployer should internalize? I say it is accountability the moment the deployer's bounded share is set high enough to bite and the backstop catches only what no tier could foresee. Set the share too low, and you are right: it becomes a subsidy. That threshold is now the whole argument.",
