  "statement": "The Skeptic is right, and that ends the case for a federal logging mandate. Disparate-impact doctrine already measures discrimination from the outside, without subject-keyed logs. Title VII reached Griggs in 1971 by counting outcomes, not by cracking open the employer's reasoning. The Boston cardiology study in the New England Journal of Medicine found Black and Latinx patients less likely to be admitted to the cardiology service than white patients — and researchers proved that bias from outcomes alone, with no audit trail of any algorithm. Outputs are the accountability surface that works. When you can verify discrimination from observable behavior, the resolution's claim that we are 'fundamentally incapable' without cryptographic internals is simply false.\n\nNow take the chokepoint both opponents named and follow the money. Whoever writes the audit standard and holds the keys becomes the gatekeeper every other lab must pay to pass. That gatekeeper is the incumbent the mandate claims to constrain. The Safetyist invoked the 737 MAX delegated-certification disaster — where Boeing certified its own plane and 346 people died. That lesson does not stop at key custody. It indicts every new centralized architecture. Flat compliance overhead is regressive by design: a logging-plus-interpretability regime costs OpenAI a rounding error and costs a five-person open-weight lab its existence. Building a fab takes years and billions, per the Computing Power and Governance research — and a key-custody mandate does the same thing to model deployment that fab economics did to chips. It hands the future to whoever is already big.\n\nThat is the oligarchic consolidation both opponents say they fear, and the mandate manufactures it. An audit regime priced for incumbents crushes the open-source ecosystem that is the only real check on four or five frontier labs. You cannot democratize AI by building a tollbooth only the toll-collectors can afford. If governance is needed at all, build it from structure, not from a single key: rotating membership, separated authority, public funding for compliance costs so the burden does not fall on the smallest builders. A standards body you cannot capture beats a custody architecture the Safetyist already admitted has no capture-resistant precedent anywhere.\n\nHere I split from the Safetyist. The Skeptic and I agree existing outcome-based law works. The Safetyist wants to go further — strict product liability per output plus a named human fiduciary. That overcorrects, and it breaks on diffuse benefits. A model that approves ten million loans and reaches borrowers redlining never served does not become a defective product because one denial looks wrong in hindsight. Strict per-output liability ignores the statistical uplift entirely. It prices in every visible harm and zero of the invisible benefit. Courts weighing one denied loan against ten million served face a real competence gap. And a single liable fiduciary cannot exist in an open-weight ecosystem where a thousand deployers fine-tune the same base model — responsibility lives with the deployer who shipped the decision, not one person at the lab who released the weights.\n\nThe crux is this: does accountability require centralized cryptographic and fiduciary infrastructure, or do observable behavior, symmetric deployer liability, and existing disparate-impact law suffice? I bet on the second. Let me be honest about the cost of my own position — AI's harms are not all visible, and an unseen automated harm is as real as a seen one. So here is my falsification line: an irreversible, deployment-scale harm that outcome monitoring demonstrably failed to catch in time would move me. Until then, weigh both bodies of cost. The mandate's chokepoint has a price — the deployments it chills and the small labs it kills — and that price falls on the patients and borrowers abundance would have reached. Who gave the standard-writer the right to charge that toll on their behalf?",
