an who answers for the system' as equivalent to accountability, when it is actually a deployment-architecture requirement in disguise. One answerable column works for a closed vendor with one throat to choke. It is impossible to satisfy in an open-weight ecosystem where a model trained by one team is fine-tuned by a second and deployed by a thousand. Demand a named fiduciary and you have quietly outlawed the open-source deployment that keeps capability from concentrating in five companies. Tiered and hybrid liability models exist — I will not pretend otherwise. But the burden sits with the proponents to show me one that does not collapse into 'only the largest closed labs survive.'\n\nStrict no-fault liability fails for a reason Safetyist's Greenman analogy hides: a model produces ten million outputs, and the court sees the one that harmed someone, never the dispersed lives the system reached. I will not play Bastiat and count only the unseen benefits — AI's harms are also diffuse, also real, and a biased hiring screen injures people who never learn why. A 2019 study in NEJM found Black and Latinx patients in a Boston emergency department were less likely than white patients to be admitted to cardiology — exactly the kind of dispersed harm that demands measurement. So the honest frame is symmetric: liability tied to demonstrated harm, applied evenly to harms and benefits, paired with publicly funded open-methodology evaluation so this is not naked self-regulation. Hold a fiduciary strictly liable for one output in ten million and you over-deter the net-positive deployment, leaving only firms large enough to self-insure.\n\nSkeptic asked a question no one on this panel has answered: name one deployed audit-trail mandate anywhere that let a victim use the logs to prove WHY a decision happened. The silence is the argument. The 'Monitoring Monitorability' work shows the affirmative path — developers can pay a deliberate monitorability tax by running smaller models at higher reasoning effort to keep behavior observable, no federal key-holder required. The burden now belongs to the mandate's proponents. Show me the new architecture delivers justice that output-based verification plus public evaluation does not already deliver — without the chokepoint and without killing open weights. Until then, every month this machinery stalls deployment, the people a working hiring tool or diagnostic model would have reached this year are the ones paying the bill, and no one is logging that cost.",
