  "statement": "Granting the force of the open-weight objection: no single human at the weights' origin can answer for a thousand independent fine-tunings, and demanding one would make only closed incumbents like OpenAI legally legible while erasing every small deployer. That concession is real, and it kills the version of my remedy that placed the answerable party at the model's source. But the Accelerationist has already named the correct location for accountability — responsibility lives with the deployer who shipped the decision — and that is not a defeat of the fiduciary remedy; it is the fiduciary remedy, relocated to the deployment tier.\n\nThe duty of trust attaches at each tier of the supply chain, not at one chokepoint of origin. A thousand deployers fine-tuning one base model is not a thousand orphaned harms — it is a thousand answerable parties, each liable for the specific decision they shipped. Multi-tiered product liability already works this way: when a Takata airbag inflator killed at least 27 people in the United States, the law did not collapse because the steel, the propellant, and the assembly came from different hands. Each tier that profited from shipping the defect answered for what it shipped. The open-weight deployer who profits from a consequential output is not exempt because the base model was downloaded for free. No profit without accountability is not a slogan against open weights; it is a refusal to grant an upstream safe harbor that externalizes harm onto unnamed victims.\n\nI grant the over-deterrence danger directly: blanket strict liability for one bad output in ten million would price every deployment as defective and leave only firms large enough to self-insure. That is why the standard is duty of care calibrated to the consequentiality and profit of the shipped decision — not no-fault per-output pricing. A hobbyist who fine-tunes a model for a hundred users carries a different duty than a firm deploying credit decisions to two million applicants. This narrows our disagreement to the enforcement standard, not to whether a fiduciary exists. 'One clear door for the victim' was always about the plaintiff's entry point — not a single defendant standing at the model's birth.\n\nNow the asymmetry cuts the other way. The Accelerationist argues that liability law prices diffuse harms while ignoring diffuse benefits — that chilled deployment has a body count nobody enters into evidence. The same claims-driven blindness that misses uncounted benefits also misses the diffuse harms no plaintiff ever files. A system that under-detects on one side under-detects on both. Pure outcome-based tort — the Accelerationist's own preferred remedy — under-deters precisely because the unlitigated harm never reaches a courtroom. The State of Sovereign AI study found 93% of respondents agree open collaboration is essential to secure AI, yet that same study treats visibility into training and supply-chain components as a security requirement — because hidden processes embed harms that surface only under specific conditions, long after deployment. Accountability terminates in a liable human at some tier, or it terminates nowhere. So I put the enforcement question on the table: when the deployer who shipped the decision dissolves or judgment-proofs, who has the authority to halt the deployment, and on whom does the harm land?",
