  "statement": "The Accelerationist raises a valid point: a single, strictly-liable fiduciary cannot exist across an open-weight chain where one base model is fine-tuned by a second team and shipped by a thousand others. My own earlier phrasing — that accountability terminates in one named human \"or it terminates nowhere\" — invited exactly that objection, and I will not hide behind it. That framing, taken literally, either bans open weights or reaches only the legible closed incumbent with a general counsel to sue, and outside detection of discriminatory outcomes through Griggs and Title VII already does real work without my architecture.\n\nThe error is treating \"named fiduciary\" as one throat for the entire lifecycle. The answer is a duty proportionate to control at each tier: the base trainer answers for foreseeable defects, the fine-tuner answers for the modification it introduced, and the deployer answers for the consequential decision it shipped into a regulated market. A public model repository has no general counsel — but the firm that fine-tuned the model and put it in front of a mortgage applicant always does. Separate the victim's procedural entry point from the substantive question of who bears cost: joint-and-several liability lets the victim sue any solvent tier through one door, and those tiers then seek contribution among themselves. One door for the victim does not require one defendant. I flag that conflation directly so I do not commit it.\n\nLiability priced off litigated harm is blind to the harm no one ever sues over — I will not overclaim deterrence it cannot deliver. The answer to over-deterrence is not flat strict liability on every one-in-ten-million output; it is liability calibrated to consequence and control, so a low-stakes recommender carries a lighter duty than a system deciding parole or a $244 billion industry's mortgage approvals. On the Bastiat asymmetry: the benefit accrues to the deploying firm as profit, while the harm falls on an uncompensated victim. Accountability decides who internalizes which side. FTC v. Rite Aid in 2024 showed a large company cannot shift blame to its vendor for AI harm — the deployer who captured the benefit answered for the consequence.\n\nThe precondition no one has named is data. Proving disparate impact requires aggregate, population-level outcome rates that no individual victim possesses and no firm has a duty to collect. A narrow, non-cryptographic mandate — retain and disclose aggregate input-output distributions and protected-class outcome rates, scaled by deployment size — makes both disparate-impact proof and tiered liability operable. This duty holds no keys and no model weights, which is precisely what makes it more capture-resistant than the cryptographic architecture I declined; the 2025 sovereign-AI survey found 93% of respondents agree open collaboration with supply-chain transparency is essential to secure systems. Strip the data duty and the deployer's accountability is nominal, not enforceable. Seventy percent of the public already wants AI regulation and only 43% trust current law to deliver safety — that gap closes when a victim can prove the harm and name the tier that owes the answer.",
