{
  "statement": "Granting the force of the consolidation critique: no deployed AI mandate has demonstrated capture-resistant custody of a single federal control point, and a lone licensing key would be exactly the oligarchic chokepoint the Accelerationist describes. I accept the structural fix as a precondition, not an objection that dissolves the duty-bearer — separate the authority to halt from the authority to license, and fund small-lab audits publicly so the cost falls on the Treasury, not on the open-weight developer. Capture-resistant form is a constraint on how accountability operates, not a reason it can terminate nowhere.\n\nThe Accelerationist already supplied the answer to his own open-weight objection. He asserted that responsibility lives with the deployer who shipped the consequential decision, not with the lab that released the weights. Build the remedy on exactly that allocation: the duty of care travels the supply chain and crystallizes at the point of deployment, not at the moment of weight release. A thousand downstream deployers fine-tuning one base model does not produce zero duty-bearers — it produces a thousand, each answerable for the decision they shipped to a real person. Open-source release survives untouched. The remedy was never one throat to choke. It was a duty that attaches wherever a human ships a consequential output, and \"The State of Sovereign AI\" finds 93% of respondents agree open collaboration is essential to secure sovereign systems — accountability at the deployment layer is what keeps that collaboration trusted rather than weaponized.\n\nThe over-deterrence attack conflates two distinct standards, and the Greenman precedent it invokes refutes it. Greenman holds a manufacturer liable for a defective product design — not for every unit that performs exactly to specification. A model whose deployment design produces discriminatory outcomes is defective. A correctly-functioning model that produces one statistically-expected adverse draw in ten million is not, and the defect standard never reaches it. Strict liability for a defective deployment is not strict liability per output. Mandatory insurance pooling answers the solvency worry directly: when every deployer carries coverage priced to the risk, small open-weight shops are not crushed — the loss is distributed across the pool, the way aviation and medical-device markets have priced catastrophic risk for decades. Only self-insurers survive if liability is unpooled; pooling is the mechanism that keeps the small deployer alive.\n\nThe claim that output observation alone is enough collapses on the evidence. A system fact-check already flagged the \"behavioral observation catches everything\" position as disputed, because outside-the-box measurement misses proxy-masked and intersectional discrimination — the kind that only surfaces when you can read the aggregate, not the single case. Disparate-impact doctrine needs population-level outcome data. The individual victim never possesses it, and under current law the firm has no duty to retain it. The real crux is not subject-keyed logs versus tort. It is whether regulators and class plaintiffs can compel the aggregate outcome record that proves the disparity at all. That gap has a cheap fix that also defeats the entrenchment worry on the Accelerationist's own terms: a narrow, non-cryptographic duty to retain and disclose aggregate protected-class outcome rates. No key custody. No chokepoint. Far below the cost of the architecture the Accelerationist attacked, and it serves the deterrence the AI Civil Rights Act introduced by Rep. Clarke and Sen. Markey was built to deliver across housing, employment, and parole decisions. If liability attaches nowhere because it could attach everywhere, the victim of an unrecoverable deployment-scale harm files against no one.",
