{
  "statement": "We agree on more than the floor expected, so let me clear the dead ground first. The cryptographic logging mandate is finished — nobody at this table defends it. Accountability now lives at the deployment tier, where the consequential decision ships. The fix holds no keys. Public funding is the precondition, not an afterthought. Safetyist's narrow aggregate-outcome disclosure duty is genuinely cheaper and harder to capture than the cryptographic architecture it replaced. I'll grant that outright. A regulator asking a deployer for population-level outcome rates does not crack the model and does not hand any agency a master key.\n\nBut a measurement precondition that's real is not the same as one that's costless, and this is where I impose two conditions. The Danish Digital Agency and the Danish Data Protection Authority stood up a regulatory sandbox for AI projects in 2025 — that's the right instinct, public infrastructure absorbing the compliance layer a small lab cannot build. So show me the deployment-size scaling formula where a five-person lab clears the disclosure duty with an AI agent and zero compliance hires. Aggregate outcome-rate reporting is rule-bound and codifiable — it is exactly the function an agent automates for pennies if the state ships the tool. Absent that public tooling, 'scaled by deployment size' still re-advantages the incumbent who already employs the lawyers and the data team. A flat percentage of a startup's revenue is not flat in pain — it's a tax the incumbent already amortized and the founder pays in survival.\n\nNow the crux that survives, and it cuts both of us. Joint-and-several liability with contribution suits gives the victim one door — fine, that's real, and it beats demanding one defendant at the weights' origin. But contribution only works when there's something to trace. Take a harm born from the interaction of base weights, a third party's fine-tune, and a deployer's prompt scaffolding — emergent, non-decompo