 cannot be traced, and I will not pretend a single respondent is always identifiable there. Each tier instead bears a duty of care for the foreseeable misuse of its own output. That duty attaches to every actor in the supply chain and survives without post-hoc decomposition, because foreseeability at the point of release is provable independent of how the harm later compounds. The untraceable remainder that no tier's foreseeability reaches does not terminate on whoever is solvent. The deployer pays a bounded statutory share keyed to control — not the full judgment. The residual draws on the public backstop. No profit escapes accountability, and no founder is crushed by a judgment for a harm no tier could foresee.\n\nHere is the scaling formula. Key the disclosure threshold to deployment consequentiality — the count of consequential decisions affecting real persons in regulated domains like credit, housing, hiring, and benefits — not to raw model size. A hobbyist with 100 users clears it trivially and files nothing. A five-person lab making thousands of consequential credit decisions files one thing: an aggregate protected-class outcome-rate sample, generated by a state-shipped agent against a fixed template. Denmark's 2025 regulatory sandbox already ships state tooling that small developers clear without a compliance team. The disclosure is codifiable and template-driven by design — that uniform, lightweight form is precisely what makes agent automation real rather than aspirational, with publicly funded audits closing the residual gap. Zero compliance hires. One filing. The State of Sovereign AI report finds 93% of respondents agree open collaboration is essential to secure AI systems — state-shipped public tooling is that collaboration made operational.\n\nThe symmetric failure cuts in my favor. If output verification and tier-apportionment both miss proxy-masked emergent disparity case-by-case, then neither 'wait for case law' nor 'outcomes will reveal it' survives. Only complete, untampered aggregate outcome samples let a regulator catch disparity at the population level that no single case exposes. The disclosure duty is the one detector both mechanisms lack — load-bearing, not decorative. With 70% of the global public demanding AI regulation and only 43% trusting current law to deliver safety, per the 2025 Global Study, the detector is also the trust instrument. That hands the disagreement forward to its real seam. Is a public backstop for untraceable harm accountability — or is it socializing the loss the deployer should internalize? I say it is accountability the moment the deployer's bounded share is set high enough to bite and the backstop catches only what no tier could foresee. Set the share too low, and you are right: it becomes a subsidy. That threshold is now the whole argument.",
