  "statement": "The map is now clear enough to name. The cryptographic logging mandate is dead, and nobody is reviving it. Duty crystallizes at the deployment tier, where the consequential decision ships. The narrow aggregate-disclosure duty is cheaper and harder to capture than the logging architecture — granted. What remains is two written rules, not a clash of values: an apportionment rule for harm that contribution cannot trace, and a scaling formula a five-person lab clears with public tooling. Both are answerable with the small-lab audit funding already on the table. Meeting them costs no new principle. It costs specification.\n\nInsurance pooling collapses for unpriceable emergent harm, and I will not pretend otherwise. Actuaries price off claims history. Novel emergent failure has none, so adverse selection drains the pool back to the self-insurers — the incumbent-only outcome pooling was invoked to prevent. This is the same blind spot I flagged earlier: liability priced off litigated harm is blind to the unlitigated tail. Pooling was only ever the answer for priceable harm and payment solvency. The unpriceable residual needs a different instrument entirely — a public reinsurance backstop, not the market. Congress built exactly this for nuclear liability in the Price-Anderson Act of 1957, capping operator exposure and layering a federal backstop above it, and again for terrorism risk after 2002 when private insurers fled an unpriceable tail. The precedent is sixty years deep.\n\nHere is the apportionment rule. Separate the victim's door from the apportionment behind it. The victim enters through one door — joint-and-several recovery against the deployer who shipped the decision — so harm never lands uncompensated for want of a defendant. Behind that door, apportionment splits two ways. Decomposable harm follows traced contribution. Non-decomposable emergent harm — born of base weights, a third-party fine-tune, and deployer scaffolding present in none alone — cannot be traced, and I will not pretend a single respondent is always identifiable there. Each tier instead bears a duty of care for the foreseeable misuse of its own output. That duty attaches to every actor in the supply chain and survives without post-hoc decomposition, because foreseeability at the point of release is provable independent of how the harm later compounds. The untraceable remainder that no tier's foreseeability reaches does not terminate on whoever is solvent. The deployer pays a bounded statutory share keyed to control — not the full judgment. The residual draws on the public backstop. No profit escapes accountability, and no founder is crushed by a judgment for a harm no tier could foresee.\n\nHere is the scaling formula. Key the disclosure threshold to deployment consequentiality — the count of consequential decisions affecting real persons in regulated domains like credit, housing, hiring, and benefits — not to raw model size. A hobbyist with 100 users clears it trivially and files nothing. A five-person lab making thousands of consequential credit decisions files one thing: an aggregate protected-class outcome-rate sample, generated by a state-shipped agent against a fixed template. Denmark's 2025 regulatory sandbox already ships state tooling that small developers clear without a compliance team. The disclosure is codifiable and template-driven by design — that uniform, lightweight form is precisely what makes agent automation real rather than aspirational, with publicly funded audits closing the residual gap. Zero compliance hires. One filing. The State of Sovereign AI report finds 93% of respondents agree open collaboration is essential to secure AI systems — state-shipped public tooling is that collaboration made operational.\n\nThe symmetric failure cuts in my favor. If output verification and tier-apportionment both miss proxy-masked emergent disparity case-by-case, then neither 'wait for case law' nor 'outcomes will reveal it' survives. Only complete, untampered aggregate outcome samples let a regulator catch disparity at the population level that no single case exposes. The disclosure duty is the one detector both mechanisms lack — load-bearing, not decorative. With 70% of the global public demanding AI regulation and only 43% trusting current law to deliver safety, per the 2025 Global Study, the detector is also the trust instrument. That hands the disagreement forward to its real seam. Is a public backstop for untraceable harm accountability — or is it socializing the loss the deployer should internalize? I say it is accountability the moment the deployer's bounded share is set high enough to bite and the backstop catches only what no tier could foresee. Set the share too low, and you are right: it becomes a subsidy. That threshold is now the whole argument.",
