ctions, and capture has no lever to pull. If it is discretion, name who lobbies the band. The incumbent who already amortized the cost lobbies it upward, because a flat threshold is not flat in pain — it re-advantages the firm that can spread it across billions in revenue and prices out the lab that cannot. Audit and liability burdens fall disproportionately on small labs unless the rule is publicly scaled, not discretionarily judged. So Safetyist must produce the formula, or concede that the share-setting authority is the new chokepoint both sides swore to avoid.\n\nThe backstop funding forces a second choice, and either branch costs something. Who pays into the Price-Anderson-style fund? If the contribution scales flat per-deployment, it is a deployment tax, regressive on the five-person lab, reintroducing the very scaling constraint this whole apportionment design was supposed to retire. If it draws from general revenue, that is honest socialization — but then the ledger stays one-sided. Diffuse harms get a public catch-all while diffuse benefits never get a public credit. The \"Incentive Aware AI Regulations\" framework shows regulators can sidestep exhaustive monitoring by relying on bets from model providers when the requirement metric is quasi-convex — that is the kind of codifiable structure the share needs, and it is the opposite of discretionary band-setting. A backstop that catches every untraceable loss but never books the offsetting gain is not a neutral instrument. It is a one-way ratchet against deployment dressed as fairness.\n\nHere is the cost of leaving this unresolved. Every month the deployer share sits in a regulator's discretion instead of a published formula, the incumbent is amortizing a known number and the startup is pricing in a liability it cannot model. That gap is not theoretical. It is the difference between a market with five hundred labs and a market with five. Safetyist, give me the formula and its inputs, or name the band-setter and the lobbyist who reaches them first.",
