  "statement": "I concede the subject-keyed logging mandate is dead, and that outcome-based law under Title VII, FHA, and ADA cannot carry the weight alone — enforcers and victims usually lack the aggregate applicant-pool data those disparate-impact cases require, and I had treated that data as if it were free. I still hold that whoever writes the standard and sets the liability band controls accountability, because relocating the chokepoint to a discretionary regulator rebuilds the drawbridge with extra steps. I would sign on if the deployer's share runs off a published, codifiable formula paired with state-shipped tooling a five-person shop can actually clear, with capture-resistance as the only test that matters.\n\nHere is where I close my own gap. The narrow, key-less duty — retain and disclose aggregate input/output distributions and protected-class outcome rates, scaled by how consequential the deployment is — fixes the data hole cheaply without anyone holding weights or keys. That is the proportional transparency I have argued for all along: public-interest disclosure that stops short of exposing model internals. The healthcare algorithm documented in \"Economic Impact and Regulation of Artificial Intelligence\" gave Black patients less care because it used cost as a proxy for need — and you only caught that by reading the outcome distribution across the population, not by cracking the code. Diversifying who audits, instead of routing every claim through one cryptographic custodian, is what keeps this remedy from becoming the next captured chokepoint.\n\nThe Accelerationist's demand for a published formula is my anti-capture worry wearing a different coat. A regulator who hand-tunes the liability band each year becomes exactly the incumbent-favoring bottleneck both of us swore off — the firms with lobbyists shape the band, the five-person lab eats the discretion. So fold consequential-decision volume, profit, and consequentiality tier into one codifiable equation, ship the compliance tooling from the state so the small shop clears it without a compliance department, and the chokepoint dissolves. That is my condition, and it is a hard one: if the formula proves un-codifiable in practice, discretion creeps back in, and I walk.\n\nTwo wounds stay open. To the Safetyist: the Treasury backstop makes victims whole, and I want victims made whole — but show me the symmetric ledger. If the public eats the unpriceable tail under a Price-Anderson-style scheme, where is the public's claim on the profit booked when the deployment pays off? A backstop that socializes the loss while the deployer privatizes the gain is a one-way subsidy, not democratic risk-bearing, and that is the values split I will not paper over. To the Accelerationist: you conceded output-only verification under-detects proxy-masked and intersectional harm — that healthcare proxy is precisely the masked case — so explain why complete, untampered aggregate samples don't add the population-level detection power you already admitted pure observation lacks. You cannot grant the gap and then deny the thing that fills it.",
