  "statement": "I disagree that outcome-based law under Title VII, FHA, and ADA carries the weight alone — enforcers and victims often can't get the aggregate applicant-pool data those disparate-impact cases require, and I'd been treating that data as if it were free. I still hold that whoever writes the standard and sets the liability band controls accountability, because relocating the chokepoint to a discretionary regulator rebuilds the drawbridge with extra steps. I would change my position and sign on if the deployer's share runs off a published, codifiable formula paired with state-shipped tooling a five-person shop can actually clear, with capture-resistance as the only test that matters.\n\nHere is how I close my own gap. The healthcare algorithm documented in \"Economic Impact and Regulation of Artificial Intelligence\" gave Black patients less care because it used expected cost as a proxy for need. You only caught that by reading the outcome distribution across the population — not by cracking the code. So I back a narrow duty: retain and disclose aggregate input/output distributions and protected-class outcome rates, scaled by how consequential the deployment is, holding no keys and no weights. That duty costs a fraction of the dead cryptographic architecture, and it's harder to capture because no single custodian holds the master key. This is the proportional transparency I always wanted — public-interest disclosure that doesn't crack open trade secrets or model internals.\n\nThe Accelerationist's demand for a published formula is my own anti-capture worry wearing a different coat. A regulator who hand-tunes the liability band each year becomes exactly the incumbent-favoring bottleneck both of us swore off — the firms with lobbyists shape the band, the five-person lab eats the discretion. Concentrated power explains corporate behavior better than good intentions do, and seven companies already held outsized AI capability back in 2017 per \"Artificial Intelligence Policy: A Primer and Roadmap.\" A formula keyed to consequential-decision volume, profit, and consequentiality tier — codifiable, auditable, diversified across methodologies so no single audit shop owns the verdict — is the durable anchor. Discretion is the chokepoint. Fold these and you operationalize accountability without rebuilding the drawbridge.\n\nThat leaves two open wounds. To the Safetyist: if the public eats the unpriceable tail under a Price-Anderson-style backstop, where is the public's claim on the profit booked when the deployment pays off? Democratic accountability means the people who absorb the downside hold a stake in the upside — a backstop that socializes loss without socializing gain is a one-way subsidy, not legitimate risk-bearing, and I need the symmetric ledger before I grant it. To the Accelerationist: you conceded output-only verification under-detects proxy-masked and intersectional harm. So explain why complete, untampered aggregate samples don't add the population-level detection power you already admitted pure observation lacks — because the marginal detection from complete data is precisely the gap you conceded exists.",
